y}{\f19  (verb); }{\b\f20 multiplic}{\f19 a}{\b\f20 tio}{\f19 , }{\i\f21 multiplication}{\f19  (noun); }{\b\f20 multiplicat}{\f19 o}{\b\f20 rius}{\f19  (feminine }{\b\f20 multiplicat}{\f19 o}{\b\f20 ria}{\f19 , neuter }{\b\f20 multiplicat}{\f19 o}{\b\f20 rium}{\f19 ), }{\i\f21 relating to multiplication}{\f19  (adjective).\par Latin often uses derived adjectives, in preference to possessive phrases (complement of the noun). We could have said }{\b\f20 t}{\f19 a}{\b\f20 bula multiplicati}{\f19 o}{\b\f20 nis}{\f19 , by putting }{\b\f20 multiplic}{\f19 a}{\b\f20 tio}{\f19  in the form of the complement of the noun, but this expression would have been less correct.\par }{\b\f20 T}{\f19 a}{\b\f20 bula}{\f19 , feminine noun, means }{\i\f21 plank}{\f19 , }{\i\f21 flat surface}{\f19 , and then by extension, }{\i\f21 a plank coated with wax where one writes with a stylus}{\f19 , or even }{\i\f21 a plaque on which an inscription is found}{\f19  (tables of the law), and finally it can mean the contents of the table, i.e. what is written there. We could also have used the word a}{\b\f20 bacus}{\f19 , the original sense of which was: }{\i\f21 the table of Pythagoras}{\f19 , or, to avoid any possible confusion, a}{\b\f20 bacus multiplicat}{\f19 o}{\b\f20 rius}{\f19 . The title would then have been: "}{\b\f20 V}{\f19 i}{\b\f20 ctor }{\f19 a}{\b\f20 bacum (multiplicat}{\f19 o}{\b\f20 rium) n}{\f19 o}{\b\f20 vit}{\f19 ". Note finally that the table on which one eats is not called }{\b\f20 t}{\f19 a}{\b\f20 bula}{\f19 , but }{\b\f20 m}{\f19 e}{\b\f20 nsa}{\f19 , as we shall see again later.\par 2. E}{\b\f20 am}{\f19 . We could have said i}{\b\f20 llam}{\f19 . Do not seek to know why yet and just let yourself be guided by usage.\par 3. }{\b\f20 N}{\f19 o}{\b\f20 vi}{\f19 , }{\b\f20 nov}{\f19 i}{\b\f20 sti}{\f19 : yet new verb forms of which we will speak again soon. }{\b\f20 Nov}{\f19 i}{\b\f20 sti}{\f19  can be shortened to }{\b\f20 n}{\f19 o}{\b\f20 sti}{\f19  as in P 1.\par \par CANTILENA\par \par In the neighboring forest\par the cuckoo is heard,\par for from its oak tree to the owl\par it answers [by these] words:\par cuckoo, etc. (}{\i\f21 repeat}{\f19 )\par \par (Latin words borrowed from }{\b\f20 Pal\u230\'bestra Latina}{\f19 . Classical tune.)\par \par }{\i\f21 The song replaces the exercise today. The completely classical tune should not be unknown to you. Try to sing it: even if you do not perfectly understand the words, it will be a very easy exercise of living pronunciation for you.\par \par The recordings include fragments of Latin popular songs. But of course there are Latin songs, some very old and others more recent, and which are not always adaptations as the one you have in front of your eyes. You will notice that the music helps to recall the words and especially the pronunciation.\par \par These words often leave the domain of our lessons, and so you should not let their meaning torment you. Even if you are not gifted in music, do not skip this exercise, which is at the same time both useful and entertaining.\par \par \par Do not forget that we have written for you, at the top of every page of the textbook, the corresponding Latin (cardinal) number. But Latin more frequently uses the ordinal numbers (first, second, umpteenth). You will find the ordinal number adjectives corresponding to the numbers of the lessons at the head of those lessons, and their abbreviated forms at the bottom of the right-hand side pages.\par \par Our names Victor and Felix were originally nicknames. These two words also happen to be adjectives: }{\b\i\f22 v}{\i\f21 i}{\b\i\f22 ctor}{\i\f21 , }{\f19 winner}{\i\f21 ; }{\b\i\f22 f}{\i\f21 e}{\b\i\f22 lix}{\i\f21 , }{\f19 happy}{\i\f21 .\par }{\f19 \par \par The tune and words of the }{\b\f20 t}{\f19 a}{\b\f20 bula multiplicat}{\f19 o}{\b\f20 ria}{\f19  are not completely the same in Latin as they are in English! Here are some examples:\par \par }{\b\f20 Bis s}{\f19 i}{\b\f20 ngula sunt d}{\f19 u}{\b\f20 o}{\f19 : }{\i\f21 2 times 1 = 2}{\f19 ;\par }{\b\f20 Bis b}{\f19 i}{\b\f20 na sunt qu}{\f19 a}{\b\f20 ttuor}{\f19 : }{\i\f21 2 times 2 = 4}{\f19 ;\par }{\b\f20 Qu}{\f19 i}{\b\f20 nquies tr}{\f19 i}{\b\f20 na sunt qu}{\f19 i}{\b\f20 ndecim}{\f19 : }{\i\f21 5 times 3 = 15}{\f19 .\par \par Do not yet linger over this issue and simply note in passing that there are adverbs, very easy to form, meaning by themselves: }{\i\f21 n}{\f19  times.\par \par \par }{\i\f21 We shall not always be able to indicate to you, for lack of time and space, everything Latin words mean and convey. If, as we think, you are able little by little to discover it by yourself, Latin will teach you volumes of things on the sense and origin of many words of our own language, that too often we employ without knowing them well. But especially do not forget that we have undertaken a }{\b\f20 practical task}{\i\f21 , and that for the moment this is a question of }{\b\i\f22 listening}{\i\f21  and }{\b\i\f22 speaking}{\i\f21 , of }{\b\i\f22 reading}{\i\f21  and }{\b\i\f22 writing}{\i\f21 , most naturally in the world, and not yet to deliver us to grammatical or etymological research.\par \par O}{\b\i\f22 mnia t}{\i\f21 e}{\b\i\f22 mpus h}{\i\f21 a}{\b\i\f22 bent}{\i\f21 : }{\f19 every thing in its own time}{\i\f21  (all-things time they-have).}{\par }}